0,Northwest Caucasian languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northwest_Caucasian_languages,"Adyghe is one of the more widely spoken Northwest Caucasian languages.Ubykh forms a third branch, with parallels to both Adyghe and Abkhaz. The population switched to speaking Adyghe, and Ubykh became extinct on 7 October 1992, with the death of Tevfik Esenç.",Adyghe,Abkhaz-Adyghe
1,Proto-Northwest Caucasian language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proto-Northwest_Caucasian_language,"Proto-Northwest Caucasian (sometimes abbreviated PNWC), also called Proto-West Caucasian (PWC) or Proto-Abkhaz-Adyghe, is the reconstructed common ancestor of the Northwest Caucasian languages.",Adyghe,Abkhaz-Adyghe
2,"Abkhazian Folk Song - Адыга Таца (Adyghe Nisa, Adyghe Taca)Proto-Northwest Caucasian language - WikipediaNorthwest Caucasian languages - WikipediaStream Adyghe Abzekh music | Listen to songs, albums ...",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nfQBvIj4UEc,"This song is an Abkhazian version of a Circassian folk song called Adyghe Nisa (Адыгэ нысэ) Used Video...more Proto-Northwest Caucasian (sometimes abbreviated PNWC), also called Proto-West Caucasian (PWC) or Proto-Abkhaz-Adyghe, is the reconstructed common ancestor of the Northwest Caucasian languages. Adyghe has many consonants: between 50 and 60 consonants in the various Adyghe dialects but it has only three phonemic vowels. Its consonants and consonant clusters are less complex than the Abkhaz–Abaza dialects. Play Adyghe Abzekh and discover followers on SoundCloud | Stream tracks, albums, playlists on desktop and mobile. How many consonants does Adyghe have? There is even a small community in the United States. Four main dialects are recognised: Temirgoy, Abadzekh, Shapsugh and Bzhedugh, as well as many minor ones such as Hakuchi spoken by the last speakers of Ubykh in Turkey. Adyghe has many consonants: between 50 and 60 consonants in the various Adyghe dialects but it has only three phonemic vowels. How many dialects are there in Abkhaz? There are two major dialects, Tapant and Ashkhar. Some are partially intelligible with Abkhaz. Ubykh forms a third branch, with parallels to both Adyghe and Abkhaz. The population switched to speaking Adyghe, and Ubykh became extinct on 7 October 1992, with the death of Tevfik Esenç. How many people speak Adyghe? Adyghe is one of the more widely spoken Northwest Caucasian languages. It has 500,000 speakers spread throughout Russia and the Middle East: 280,000 in Turkey; 125,000 in Russia, where it is official in the Republic of Adygea; 45,000 in Jordan, 25,000 in Syria, 20,000 in Iraq, and 4,000 in Israel. Is Abkhaz a palatalized or labialized vowel? Abkhaz is characterised by unusual consonant clusters and one of the world's smallest vowel inventories: It has only two distinctive vowels, an open vowel /a/ and a mid vowel /ə/. Next to palatalized or labialized consonants, /a/ is realized as [e] or [o], and /ə/ as [i] or [u].",Adyghe,Abkhaz-Adyghe
3,"Anthology of Adyghe Symphonic Music: 1922-2022"" Project ...",https://abkhazworld.com/aw/art-and-living/2209-anthology-of-adyghe-symphonic-music-1922-2022,"Mar 8, 2023 · The project preserved and immortalized a large music collection written in the first half of the 20th century in Adyghea. This will allow everyone to get acquainted with the origins of national academic music and the first attempts at composition.",Adyghe,Abkhaz-Adyghe
